<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger Casement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger Casement was an Irish-born British consul whose 1904 report on atrocities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, and later on the Putumayo region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, made him a leading humanitarian. Turning to Irish nationalism, he sought German help for an Irish rising, was arrested on landing in Ireland in 1916 and was hanged for treason in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-casement.jpg" alt="Roger Casement" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Internet Archive Book Images · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrated_spies_and_famous_mysteries_of_the_great_war_(1919)_(14765772825).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roger David Casement was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 1 September 1864. His mother, Anne Jephson, had him secretly baptised as a Catholic at the age of three, but he was raised a Protestant and remained one as an adult. In 1884 he went to the Congo to work for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> and the African International Association, a front for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a>. He helped survey and build a railway to bypass the cataracts of the lower Congo, and learned African languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Casement became a British consul in 1891. His Casement Report on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> was honoured in 1905. In 1906 the Foreign Office sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and there he joined a commission investigating an enslaved workforce collecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> for the Peruvian Amazon Company, a firm registered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He was knighted in 1911 for his investigations of abuses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> and the colonial atrocities he investigated made him mistrust <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, and he retired from consular service in 1913.</p><p>He joined the Gaelic League in 1904 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> in 1905. In July 1914 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to raise money for the Irish Volunteers and helped organise and finance the Howth gun-running. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he sought German military aid for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916, but believed the Germans were giving inadequate aid that doomed a rising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Casement's trial opened on 26 June 1916. The Treason Act 1351 seemed to cover only acts on English soil, and the court read a comma into its text so that in the realm or elsewhere described where acts were done. Casement wrote that he was to be hanged on a comma. He was hanged on 3 August 1916 and buried in quicklime at Pentonville Prison, though he had wished to lie at Murlough Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a>. British governments refused requests for the return of his remains for decades.</p><p>In 1916 British officials showed typescripts said to copy private journals, the Black Diaries, which described homosexual activity, to influential people to undermine support for clemency. Whether the diaries are genuine has been much disputed: Roger McHugh and Angus Mitchell regard them as forged, while Jeffrey Dudgeon finds the evidence convincing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roger Casement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…whose photographs of mutilated people were shown at meetings across Britain and America, publicised the atrocities; the British consul <b>Roger Casement</b>’s report of 1904 gave them official weight; and Edmund Dene Morel’s Congo Reform Association, founded in 1904, built one of the first mass international human rights movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Congo Free State Leopold II’s agents enforced rubber quotas by hostage-taking, mutilation and killing, as the British consul <b>Roger Casement</b> reported in 1904.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in HM Prison Pentonville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.55,-0.12&amp;year=1897" aria-label="HM Prison Pentonville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Roger Casement"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4994 5720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4994 5720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.55,-0.12&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
